ama  administration had put out a few white  papers on the future of AI and big data  raising some concerns but also raising  you know the promising possibilities on  the other hand the Trump administration  Trump campaign sort of on a bring jobs  back to America platform which may or  may not conflict with AI so there are  some questions there although I don't  think we've seen him take much action  either way with regards to big data and  AI local laws so I was really interested  to see that New York City is actually  trying to pass some legislation to  compel agencies when they use AI by  which I mean any automated  decision-making to release the source  code and also to permit people to test  the behavior of those algorithms so  essentially they're saying that Computer  Fraud and Abuse Act we don't want that  to stop research into whether agencies  are using discriminatory software or  even just bad software so they are  trying to enable that of course there  has been  back from industry which is fair right  it's not fair to require people to give  away their IP but local sophisticated  jurisdictions are beginning to look into  solutions on the more outlandish sort of  idea about how we're gonna deal with  this there are also things like basic  income experiments right so there are  some like very far-reaching far-seeing  sorts of things going on in terms of how  are we going to deal with this and then  I've really been excited in the last  year or two I think we are seeing more  technical solutions right so I don't  think we're seeing legal solutions but  we are seeing a lot of researchers and  computer science and statistics looking  into how can we ensure that our models  are fair right and can we write a  general framework to look for  discrimination so this is fair test  Columbia put this out last year it's  very easy to integrate it's written in  Python it works very nicely with pandas  there are also technical solutions for  correcting results so these tw